{"id":"ak-74","slug":"ak-74","name":"AK-74","aliases":["AK74","AKSU","AK 74","Ak74m","AKS-74","AK74SU","AKM 74","AK-74M","AK 74u","Ak-74u","AKS74U","AK-74su","AKS 74U","AKSU 74","AKSU-74","AKSU-47","Krinkov","AK-47SU","Ak 74 Su","AKS-74UB","MPi-AK-74","MPi-AKS-74","MPi-AKS-74K","AKS-74 Assault Rifle"],"kind":"firearm","category":"assault-rifles","subcategory":"1974 Soviet 5.45×39mm assault rifle","description":"The AK-74 is an assault rifle designed by small arms designer Mikhail Kalashnikov in 1974 as a successor to the AKM. It is chambered for the 5.45×39mm cartridge, which replaced the 7.62×39mm cartridge of Kalashnikov's earlier automatic weapons for the Soviet Armed Forces.","development":"The AK-74 was designed by А. D. Kryakushin's group under the design supervision of Mikhail Kalashnikov. It is an adaptation of the 7.62×39mm AKM assault rifle and features several important design improvements. These improvements were primarily the result of converting the rifle to the intermediate-calibre high velocity 5.45×39mm cartridge. In fact, some early models are reported to have been converted AKMs, re-barreled to 5.45×39mm. Compared with the preceding AKM, the AK-74 has better effective firing range, accuracy (a main development goal), and reliability. About 50% of the parts in the AK-74 and AKM are the same (including pins, springs and screws).","facts":[],"specifications":[{"group":"Cartridge & Action","items":[{"label":"Cartridge","value":"5.45×39mm"},{"label":"Action","value":"Gas-operated, long-stroke piston, closed rotating bolt"},{"label":"Rate of Fire (Cyclic)","value":"650 rounds/min","numeric":650,"unit":"rounds/min"},{"label":"Rate of Fire (Practical)","value":"100 rounds/min","numeric":100,"unit":"rounds/min"},{"label":"Muzzle Velocity","value":"880–900 m/s"},{"label":"Feed System","value":"30-round AK-74 and 45-round RPK-74 detachable box magazine, 60-round casket magazine and 96-round RPK-16 drum magazines"},{"label":"Sights","value":"Adjustable iron s